Accuracy (Acc) = (1 - (|True Value - Measured Value| / |True Value|))

In LaTeX code, you can write this equation as:

\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}

Accuracy (Acc) = \left(1 - \left| \frac{\left| true value - measured value \right|}{\left| true value \right|} \right| \right)

\end{document}

This will produce the following output:

Accuracy (Acc) = (1 - (|true value - measured value| / |true value|))

Note that I've used the `|` symbol to represent absolute values, and the `\left` and `\right` commands to ensure that the parentheses and absolute value symbols are properly sized.

Alternatively, you can use the ` inaccurate` package to define a `\accuracy` command that can be used to typeset the equation. Here's an example:

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{inaccurate}

\begin{document}

\accuracy{true value}{measured value}

\end{document}

This will produce the same output as the previous example, but with the added benefit of automatically generating the correct spacing and formatting

